oks and it's working with  the durham regional police service to  determine who is responsible  the principal says there will be  disciplinary action  the outlook for oil demand coming up new  projections paint an  uncertain future for alberta's oil patch  plus  record breaker the astonishing flight  that just landed one bird  in the history books  there was a stark picture painted today  by the international energy agency  it says the era of global demand for oil  will come to an  end in the next decade the agency  believes the pandemic is not just a  short-term shock but a long-lasting blow  to the industry  and that demand for oil will peak by  2030 as people and countries seek  lower emission ways of generating power  and that will have profound  consequences for oil-rich economies like  alberta  heather urex west has reaction from  there tonight  to say the last few years have been  bumpy for the alberta oil patch  is to put it mildly job losses have been  massive and the province is facing  record levels of debt  particularly for oil we saw in 2028  drop in demand for oil as people were  staying home not flying a huge drop  lifestyles are changing now a new  forecast from the international energy  agency  is suggesting that some of those changes  are not coming back  growth in global oil demand is set to  end within 10 years  but those growth projections have been  greatly impacted by covet 19.  pre-pandemic oil demand was set to rise  12 percent by 2030  that's now fallen to 9 percent but if  coveted 19 isn't brought under control  by next year  that forecast falls all the way to just  four percent growth  to a certain degree it's sort of a  confirmation of writings on the wall  uh that others have been pointing to for  some time  it means alberta's energy sector will  need to fight harder for fewer  opportunities  and that's something industry says  canada has not been successful with so  far  today we're losing investment to norway  to offshore brazil to the middle east  that competition isn't going to go away  and in a lower growth scenario  i think we need to be more ambitious  more aggressive alberta's government  says despite adjusted growth projections  the report  shows oil and gas demand is not  disappearing anytime  soon that said we also know that there's  new sources of energy coming into the  mix we brought forward a  natural gas strategy last week to focus  on hydrogen petrochemicals plastics  circular economy for plastics  geothermal we're looking at small  modular reactors  there is some hopeful news in the report  post pandemic oil prices are expected to  surge as the industry plays catch up  with global demand  but long term a massive shift has begun  leaving energy producing nations like  canada with few choices  make changes now or risk being left  behind  heather urex west global news calgary  no lines no problem ahead one tourist  who got a world heritage site all to  himself  it is not news that birds migrate of  course but the journey this one takes is  simply astonishing and it set a new  record  for non-stop flight a bartailed godwit  was tracked as it migrated from alaska  to new zealand  it flew up to 88 kilometers an hour and  it traveled over 12  000 kilometers in just 11 days without  stopping  to eat drink or sleep that beats the  previous record when a godwit was  tracked covering more than 11 000  kilometers in nine days  godwits weigh on average less than a  pound and scientists still don't fully  understand how they navigate over the  open water of the pacific ocean  well it took a pandemic to give one  adventurer the opportunity of a lifetime  peruvian tourism officials opened  machu picchu for the first time in seven  months just for this one japanese  tourist  jesse katayama arrived to see the famous  inca ruins on march 14th  but two days later peru went into  lockdown and the site was shut down  he stayed in the area though and local  tourism authorities  found out about his story and they  awarded his patients with  this pristine view of the site which  would normally be crowded with thousands  of visitors  machu picchu isn't expected to open to  larger number of tourists  until next month next the tech savvy  teens who developed a new haven for  dementia patients  with so many negative things happening  around the world it's always a plus when  we can shine a light on the people  making a positive difference  this story is about a group of teenage  girls from ireland who entered an  international tech competition  they created an app with a personal  connection and not only did they win the  top prize  it promises to improve the lives of  people living with dementia  mike drolet reports  when you give out an award virtually you  lose that element of excitement  but you have to think the us-based hosts  of the technovation awards felt the  earth move  when three nigerian irish girls from  drahida ireland  took home the top prize memory haven  [Applause]  that by the way is how it's done  celebrate when you win  apologize on national broadcaster rte  later  i'm pretty sure we'd start the neighbors  to be honest like probably got a couple  noise complaints  but yeah it was just unreal yeah it's  pretty unreal when you beat out 1500  apps created by students from 62  countries and when their mentor's mother  died from dementia they honored her by  creating  memory haven which acts as a surrogate  memory for patients  while keeping them connected with family  we created an app to promote the  independence of people living with  dementia  it was a runaway hit and the  overwhelming response was positive  but there's always a but the biggest  moment of their young lives  was also they say their first exposure  to racism  their mentor evelyn nemayo tried to  shield them from the hate  i remember the night after i saw it i  was just going through it i actually  cried  especially when i saw the monkey face  and  you know i i sorry i'm getting emotio